[\n   2x + 5y = 2 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x - 2y = 7 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiply Equation 2 by 5 to align the \\(y\\) coefficients:\n   \\[\n   5(x - 2y) = 5(7) \\implies 5x - 10y = 35 \\quad \\text{(Equation 3)}\n   \\]\n   \n   Subtract Equation 1 from Equation 3 to eliminate \\( y \\):\n   \\[\n   5x - 10y - (2x + 5y) = 35 - 2 \\implies 5x - 10y - 2x - 5y = 33 \\implies 3x - 15y = 33\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3x = 33 \\implies x = \\frac{33}{3} = 11\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute \\( x = 11 \\) back into Equation 2:\n   \\[\n   11 - 2y = 7 \\implies -2y = 7 - 11 \\implies -2y = -4 \\implies y = \\frac{4}{2} = 2\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, using a correct substitution yields:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{13}{3}, \\, y = -\\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, \n\\( x + y = \\frac{13}{3} - \\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{9}{3} = 3 \\).\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n\\[\\boxed{3}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given \\( 0 < \\alpha < \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) and \\( 0 < \\beta < \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\), prove that \\( \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\alpha} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 \\alpha \\sin^2 \\beta \\cos^2 \\beta} \\geq 9 \\). Additionally, determine the values of \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\) for which equality holds.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that this expression involving trigonometric functions is greater than or equal to 9, and also find when equality holds. Let me start by writing down the inequality again to make sure I have it right:\n\nGiven \\( 0 < \\alpha < \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) and \\( 0 < \\beta < \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\), prove that\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\alpha} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 \\alpha \\sin^2 \\beta \\cos^2 \\beta} \\geq 9\n\\]\nand find the values of \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\) for equality.\n\nHmm, so the left-hand side (LHS) has two terms. The first term is \\( \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\alpha} \\), which is familiar; that's the same as \\( \\sec^2 \\alpha \\). The