 hour earlier. Therefore, Car\u7532 must be faster.\n   - Let's express $v_\u4e59$ in terms of $v_\u7532$:\n     \\[\n     v_\u4e59 = \\frac{300}{t_\u7532 + 2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     v_\u7532 = \\frac{300}{t_\u7532}\n     \\]\n     Therefore:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{300}{t_\u7532} = v_\u7532 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{300}{t_\u7532 + 2} = v_\u4e59 \n     \\]\n\n5. **Finding the Average Speed**:\n   - Let\u2019s express the difference in speeds:\n     \\[\n     v_\u7532 - v_\u4e59 = \\frac{300}{t_\u7532} - \\frac{300}{t_\u7532 + 2}\n     \\]\n     Simplifying the difference using a common denominator:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{300}{t_\u7532} - \\frac{300}{t_\u7532 + 2} = 300 \\left ( \\frac{1}{t_\u7532} - \\frac{1}{t_\u7532 + 2} \\right )\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 300 \\left( \\frac{(t_\u7532+2) - t_\u7532}{t_\u7532(t_\u7532 + 2)} \\right ) = 300 \\left( \\frac{2}{t_\u7532(t_\u7532 + 2)} \\right )\n     \\]\n\n6. **Time and Speed Relationship**:\n   - Given that Car\u7532 catches up with Car\u4e59 exactly at a distance where they met, this implies:\n     \\[\n     v_\u7532 t = v_\u4e59(t + 1) \\quad \\text{(where t is the time when they met)}\n     \\]\n     and we are given the cars have different timeframes reaching within 1 hour beyond the city's distance. To simplify it down further:\n     \\[\n     t = 2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     v_\u4e59 = \\frac{300}{t_\u7532 + 2}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion**:\n   - Comparing these distances traveled, we find:\n   \\[\n   300 - 150 = 150 \\text{ km}\n   \\]\n   Hence, Car\u7532 catches Car\u4e59 at a position which is 150 km away from city $B$.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{150 \\text{ km}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Two people, A and B, depart simultaneously from the same location in opposite directions. After one hour, they reach their respective destinations, $A$ and $B$. If they start again from the original location 