mbers (neighbors are those sharing a common side). We need to find the number of such successful arrangements.\n\nFirst, let's make sure I understand the problem correctly. We have a grid where each cell is 1 or -1. For the arrangement to be successful, every cell must satisfy the condition that its value is the product of all its adjacent cells (up, down, left, right). That means, for each cell, if I multiply all its neighbors together, I should get the value of the cell itself.\n\nLet me start with small cases to see if I can find a pattern.\n\nLet's consider n=1. Then the grid size is (2^1 -1)x(2^1 -1) = 1x1. So there's only one cell. What's the condition here? The cell must be equal to the product of its neighbors. But a 1x1 grid has no neighbors. So the product of no numbers... Hmm, what is the product of an empty set? By convention, the product of an empty set is 1 (since 1 is the multiplicative identity). So the single cell must be equal to 1. Therefore, there's only 1 successful arrangement. So for n=1, the answer is 1.\n\nNow n=2. The grid size is (2^2 -1)x(2^2 -1) = 3x3. So a 3x3 grid. Each cell must be the product of its neighbors. Let's try to see what constraints this imposes.\n\nLet me visualize the 3x3 grid:\n\na b c\nd e f\ng h i\n\nEach of these a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i is either 1 or -1. Each one must be equal to the product of its neighbors.\n\nLet's start with the center cell e. Its neighbors are b, d, f, h. So e = b * d * f * h.\n\nSimilarly, take corner cell a. Its neighbors are b and d. So a = b * d.\n\nSimilarly, corner cell c has neighbors b and f, so c = b * f.\n\nCorner cell g has neighbors d and h, so g = d * h.\n\nCorner cell i has neighbors f and h, so i = f * h.\n\nEdge cell b has neighbors a, c, e. Wait, edge cell b is in the top middle. Its neighbors are a, c, and e. Wait, no, in a grid, each edge cell (but not corner) has four neighbors? Wait, no. Wait, in a 3x3 grid, the edge cells (not corners) have three neighbors. For example, 