ure. For example, a 7x7 grid can be divided into a center 3x3 grid and surrounding layers. If the center 3x3 grid must be all 1s, then the next layer would be determined by the center, and so on.\n\nAlternatively, consider that in such a grid, the number of variables and equations might lead to a unique solution. However, since each cell's value is determined by its neighbors, and the grid has no boundary (except the outermost layer), the system might be over-constrained, leading to only the trivial solution.\n\nAnother angle: in such a grid, the only solution is all 1s because any -1 would create a contradiction when propagating through the equations. For instance, if a cell is -1, its neighbors must multiply to -1. But each neighbor is also determined by its own neighbors, and this could lead to an inconsistency.\n\nIn the 3x3 case, we saw that setting any cell to -1 leads to contradictions. This might generalize to larger grids. Suppose there's a cell set to -1 somewhere in the grid. Then, its neighbors must multiply to -1, meaning an odd number of them are -1. However, those neighbors are also part of other cells' equations, which would impose further constraints. This chain reaction might propagate through the grid, eventually requiring an even number of -1s in some context where an odd number is needed, leading to a contradiction.\n\nAnother thought: the problem resembles the Ising model in statistical mechanics, where spins (which can be \u00b11) interact with their neighbors. However, in this case, the condition is stricter: each spin must be exactly the product of its neighbors. This is a deterministic condition rather than a probabilistic one.\n\nIf we think of the grid as a graph where each node must satisfy \u03c3_i = \u03a0_{j~i} \u03c3_j, then multiplying all such equations together gives \u03a0_i \u03c3_i = \u03a0_i \u03a0_{j~i} \u03c3_j. The right-hand side is \u03a0_j \u03c3_j^{degree(j)}. Therefore, \u03a0_i \u03c3_i = \u03a0_j \u03c3_j^{degree(j)}