B, the tangent at M is parallel to AB. Similarly, the tangent at N is parallel to AC. Therefore, the angles between line MN and the tangents at M and N are equal to the angles between line MN and chords AB and AC.\n\nSince the tangents at M and N are parallel to AB and AC, the angles between MN and AB, and MN and AC are equal. Let's denote these angles as \u03b8.\n\nNow, in triangles AMD and ANE:\n\n- Angle at M: angle AMD = 180\u00b0 - angle AMB - angle \u03b8. Since triangle AMB is isosceles, angle AMB = 180\u00b0 - 2 angle BAM.\n\n- Similarly, angle at N: angle ANE = 180\u00b0 - angle ANC - angle \u03b8. Angle ANC = 180\u00b0 - 2 angle CAN.\n\nBut angle BAM and angle CAN are not necessarily equal unless AB = AC, which isn't given. Therefore, this approach might not work.\n\nHowever, since MA = MB and NA = NC, by the Law of Sines in triangles AMD and ANE:\n\nIn triangle AMD: AD / sin(angle AMD) = MA / sin(angle ADM)\n\nIn triangle ANE: AE / sin(angle ANE) = NA / sin(angle AEN)\n\nAssuming angle ADM = angle AEN (since both are angles between MN and the respective chords AB, AC, which are equal due to the parallel tangents), and MA = NA if arcs AB and AC are equal. But since arcs AB and AC might not be equal, MA \u2260 NA.\n\nWait, but angle AMD and angle ANE might be supplementary or something due to the cyclic quadrilateral.\n\nAlternatively, since line MN connects M and N, and MA = MB, NA = NC, perhaps quadrilateral AMBN is cyclic. But AMBN would already be on the original circle, so that doesn't help.\n\nGiven the time I've invested and the various methods I've tried, I think the most straightforward way is to use coordinate geometry with a general case and show that AD = AE algebraically.\n\nLet\u2019s proceed with that.\n\nLet the circle be the unit circle centered at the origin, and point A at (1,0). Let points B and C be at angles \u03b8 and \u03c6, respectively. Then:\n\nCoordinates of B: (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8)\n\nCoordinates of C: (cos \u03c6, sin \u03c6