zero here, so the slope is irrational. Now, if a line has an irrational slope and passes through (a,0), can it contain another rational point?\n\nSuppose there is another rational point (p', q') on the same line. Then the slope between (a,0) and (p', q') must also be equal to the slope between (a,0) and (p, q). So, q'/(p' - a) = q/(p - a). Let's rearrange this: q'(p - a) = q(p' - a). Expanding both sides: q'p - q'a = qp' - q a. Then, bringing terms with a to one side: -q'a + q a = qp' - q'p. Factor out a: a(q - q') = qp' - q'p. The left side is a times (q - q'), and the right side is a rational number because p, p', q, q' are all rational. So, if q \u2260 q', then we can solve for a: a = (qp' - q'p)/(q - q'). But the right side is a rational number (since numerator and denominator are rational), which would contradict the fact that a is irrational. Therefore, this equation can hold only if both sides are zero. So either q - q' = 0, which would mean q = q', and then the left side is zero, so the right side must also be zero: qp' - q'p = 0. But if q = q', then this becomes q(p' - p) = 0. Since q \u2260 0 (because we assumed the point (p, q) has q \u2260 0), then p' = p. Therefore, the only way for two distinct rational points (p, q) and (p', q') to lie on the same line through (a,0) with irrational slope is if q = q' and p = p', meaning they are the same point. Therefore, such a line can have at most one rational point unless the slope is zero, which is the x-axis.\n\nWait, but the x-axis is a special case. If the line is the x-axis, then it's y=0, and it contains infinitely many rational points, like (r,0) for any rational r. But since a is irrational, (a,0) is on the x-axis. So the x-axis is one line through (a,0) that has infinitely many rational points. Then, any other line through (a,0) with a different slope can have at most one rational point. Therefore, only the x-axis has infinitely many, and all other lines have at most one. So in that case, how does this affect the options?\n\nLooking back at the options:\n\nA. Infinitely many lines each have at least two rational points. But we just saw that only the x-axis has infinitely many, but all other lines have at most one. So A is false.\n\nB. Exactly n lines (finite number) each have at least two. But in this case, only the x-axis has infinitely many, but maybe other lines? Wait, but if a line has two rational points, then it must be the x-axis. Wait, if a line is not the x-axis, can it have even two rational points?\n\nWait, earlier reasoning: if a line passes through (a,0) and another rational point (p, q), then any other rational point on that line would have to satisfy a = (qp' - q'p)/(q - q'). Since a is irrational, this can't happen unless q = q' and p = p', so only one rational point. Therefore, any line not the x-axis can have at most one rational point. The x-axis has infinitely many. Therefore, the only line through (a,0) with more than one rational point is the x-axis. Therefore, there's exactly one line (the x-axis) that contains infinitely many rational points, which certainly includes at least two. So option C says \"exactly one line that contains at least two rational points.\" That would be true, right? Because only the x-axis has infinitely many, which is at least two. All other lines have at most one. Therefore, C is correct?\n\nWait, but wait. Suppose there is another line, not the x-axis, that passes through (a,0) and another rational point. Wait, but according to the earlier reasoning, such a line can't have two distinct rational points. Because if it did, that would force a to be rational, which it isn't. So any non-x-axis line through (a,0) can have at most one rational point. So the only line that has at least two rational points is the x-axis, hence exactly one line (option C). So the answer should be C.\n\nBut wait, let's check again. Suppose someone claims that maybe there's another horizontal line, but no, a horizontal line other than y=0 would have equation y = c, where c \u2260 0. If such a line passes through (a,0), then 0 = c, which is a contradiction. So the only horizontal line is y=0. Similarly, vertical lines through (a,0) would be x = a, which is a vertical line. But x=a has no rational points because a is irrational. So the only points on x=a are (a, y), but y would have to be irrational for the point to be rational, which it can't. So x=a has no rational points. So vertical line is out.\n\nOther lines: suppose a line with rational slope? Wait, but if the line passes through (a,0), can it have a rational slope? Let's say the line is y = m(x - a), where m is the slope. If m is rational, then can this line have any rational points? Suppose (p, q) is a rational point on this line. Then q = m(p - a). Since m is rational, and p is rational, then m(p - a) is rational only if m(p - a) is rational. But a is irrational, so p - a is irrational, and multiplied by m (rational non-zero) gives another irrational number. Then q would have to be irrational, but q is rational. Contradiction. Therefore, a line with rational slope passing through (a,0) cannot contain any rational points except possibly if q = 0. But q = 0 would require m(p - a) = 0, which since m \u2260 0 (unless it's the x-axis), then p = a, which is irrational. Therefore, such a line would have no rational points. Therefore, lines with rational slope through (a,0) (other than the x-axis) have no rational points.\n\nWhat about lines with irrational slope? Wait, earlier we considered lines passing through (a,0) and a rational point (p, q). The slope is q/(p - a), which is rational divided by irrational, so irrational. So such lines have irrational slopes, and as we saw, they can contain at most one rational point. So even if a line has an irrational slope, it can have at most one rational point.\n\nTherefore, the only line through (a,0) that has infinitely many rational points is the x-axis. All other lines through (a,0) have at most one rational point. Therefore, there is exactly one line (the x-axis) that contains at least two rational points. So the correct answer is C.\n\nWait, but let's check if the x-axis is the only one. Suppose we have a line that is not the x-axis, but still contains two rational points. Wait, but according to our earlier reasoning, that would require a to be expressible as a rational combination, which would make a rational, contradiction. Hence, the x-axis is indeed the only line with at least two rational points. Therefore, answer C.\n\nBut wait, let's think of an example. Let\u2019s take a = sqrt(2), which is irrational. Consider the x-axis: it's y=0, passes through (sqrt(2),0), and has infinitely many rational points like (1,0), (2,0), etc. Now take another line, say passing through (sqrt(2),0) and (1,1). The slope is (1 - 0)/(1 - sqrt(2)) = 1/(1 - sqrt(2)) = -(1 + sqrt(2))/( (1)^2 - (sqrt(2))^2 ) = -(1 + sqrt(2))/(-1) = 1 + sqrt(2), which is irrational. Now, can this line have another rational point? Suppose there's another rational point (p, q) on this line. Then we have q = (1 + sqrt(2))(p - sqrt(2)). Let's expand this: q = (1 + sqrt(2))p - (1 + sqrt(2))sqrt(2) = (1 + sqrt(2))p - sqrt(2) - 2. Rearranging: q = (1 + sqrt(2))p - (sqrt(2) + 2). Now, since p and q are rational, the left side is rational. The right side has sqrt(2) terms. To have the right side rational, the coefficients of sqrt(2) must cancel out. Let's write it as:\n\nRight side = [1*p - 2] + [sqrt(2)*p - sqrt(2)] = (p - 2) + sqrt(2)(p - 1).\n\nFor this to be rational, the coefficient of sqrt(2) must be zero. So sqrt(2)(p - 1) must be zero, which implies p - 1 = 0, so p = 1. Then substituting back, q = (1 + sqrt(2))(1) - sqrt(2) - 2 = 1 + sqrt(2) - sqrt(2) - 2 = 1 - 2 = -1. So the only rational point on this line is (1, -1)? Wait, but we already had (1,1) on the line. Wait, no, (1,1) was the point we started with. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me check. If the line passes through (sqrt(2), 0) and