 + (-189) =81 +108 -189= 189 -189=0\n\nWait, that's 81 +108 -189= 189 -189=0. So that works. Wait, why did I compute it as 81 -108 -189 before? That was incorrect. Because the second term is - (8 +4x)y, which is -(-108)=+108, and the third term is +(-189). So total is 81 +108 -189=0. So it does satisfy. So my previous calculation was wrong. So x=-5, y=9 is valid.\n\nSimilarly, check x=0:\n\nCompute y=(0 +0 +16)/4=16/4=4\n\nCheck first equation: y\u00b2=16. Right side: (0 +8)(0 +2)=8*2=16. So 16=16. Valid.\n\nSecond equation: y\u00b2 - (8 +0)y + (16 +0 -0)=16 -8*4 +16=16 -32 +16=0. So yes, 0. Valid.\n\nx=19:\n\nCompute y=(19\u00b2 +19 +16)/4=(361 +19 +16)/4=(396)/4=99\n\nCheck first equation: y\u00b2=99\u00b2=9801\n\nRight side: (19 +8)(19\u00b2 +2)=27*(361 +2)=27*363=9801. So 9801=9801. Valid.\n\nSecond equation:\n\ny\u00b2 - (8 +4*19)y + (16 +16*19 -5*19\u00b2)\n\nCompute:\n\ny\u00b2=9801\n\n(8 +76)y=84y=84*99=8316\n\n(16 +304 -5*361)=16 +304 -1805=320 -1805= -1485\n\nSo equation becomes:\n\n9801 -8316 + (-1485)= (9801 -8316)=1485; 1485 -1485=0. Valid.\n\nSo all three solutions x=-5,0,19 are valid, as well as x=-2 with y=\u00b16.\n\nWait, so total solutions are:\n\nx=-2, y=6; x=-2, y=-6; x=-5, y=9; x=0, y=4; x=19, y=99.\n\nBut wait, let's check x=-2 in the expression y=(x\u00b2 +x +16)/4.\n\nIf x=-2, then y=(4 -2 +16)/4=(18)/4=4.5. But earlier, we found y=\u00b16. So if x=-2, y=\u00b16, which contradicts y=4.5. Hence, x=-2 is a separate solution not covered by the y=(x\u00b2 +x +16)/4 expression.\n\nThus, all solutions are:\n\n1. x=-2, y=6\n\n2. x=-2, y=-6\n\n3. x=-5, y=9\n\n4. x=0, y=4\n\n5. x=19, y=99\n\nTherefore, the system has five solutions.\n\nBut let me confirm once more, perhaps with a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, the second equation is a quadratic in y. Maybe we can solve for y using quadratic formula and then set equal to the first equation.\n\nSecond equation: y\u00b2 - (8 +4x)y + (16 +16x -5x\u00b2) =0\n\nUse quadratic formula:\n\ny = [ (8 +4x) \u00b1 sqrt( (8 +4x)^2 -4*1*(16 +16x -5x^2) ) ] /2\n\nCompute discriminant:\n\nD = (8 +4x)^2 -4*(16 +16x -5x\u00b2)\n\nExpand (8 +4x)^2:\n\n64 +64x +16x\u00b2\n\nThen subtract 4*(16 +16x -5x\u00b2):\n\n=64 +64x +16x\u00b2 -64 -64x +20x\u00b2\n\nSimplify:\n\n(64 -64) + (64x -64x) + (16x\u00b2 +20x\u00b2)= 0 +0 +36x\u00b2=36x\u00b2\n\nSo discriminant D=36x\u00b2\n\nTherefore, solutions for y are:\n\ny = [ (8 +4x) \u00b1 sqrt(36x\u00b2) ] /2 = [ (8 +4x) \u00b16|x| ] /2\n\nBut since sqrt(36x\u00b2)=6|x|, but maybe we can write it as 6x if x\u22650, -6x if x<0. However, perhaps it's better to keep as \u00b16x.\n\nWait, actually sqrt(36x\u00b2)=6|x|, but when we write \u00b1sqrt(D), it's \u00b16|x|. But since we have \u00b1 in front, maybe we can combine:\n\n[ (8 +4x) \u00b16|x| ] /2\n\nSo depending on the sign of x, |x| is x or -x. Let's consider cases for x \u22650 and x <0.\n\nCase 1: x \u22650\n\nThen |x|=x, so:\n\ny = [8 +4x \u00b16x]/2\n\nTwo possibilities:\n\na) y = [8 +4x +6x]/2 = [8 +10x]/2=4 +5x\n\nb) y = [8 +4x -6x]/2 = [8 -2x]/2=4 -x\n\nCase 2: x <0\n\nThen |x|=-x, so:\n\ny = [8 +4x \u00b16*(-x)]/2\n\nWhich is:\n\na) y = [8 +4x -6x]/2 = [8 -2x]/2=4 -x\n\nb) y = [8 +4x +6x]/2= [8 +10x]/2=4 +5x\n\nWait, interestingly, regardless of the sign of x, the two solutions are y=4 +5x and y=4 -x. So regardless of x's sign, the solutions for y are y=4 +5x and y=4 -x. So maybe that's a better way to write it: y=4 +5x or y=4 -x.\n\nTherefore, from the second equation, y can be 4 +5x or 4 -x. Then, substitute these into the first equation and solve for x.\n\nSo this might be a more straightforward approach.\n\nSo, substitute y=4 +5x into the first equation:\n\n(4 +5x)^2 = (x +8)(x\u00b2 +2)\n\nSimilarly, substitute y=4 -x into the first equation:\n\n(4 -x)^2 = (x +8)(x\u00b2 +2)\n\nThus, we can split into two cases.\n\nFirst, case 1: y=4 +5x\n\nPlug into first equation:\n\n(4 +5x)^2 = (x +8)(x\u00b2 +2)\n\nExpand left side:\n\n16 +40x +25x\u00b2\n\nRight side:\n\nx\u00b3 +8x\u00b2 +2x +16\n\nSet equal:\n\n25x\u00b2 +40x +16 =x\u00b3 +8x\u00b2 +2x +16\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n-x\u00b3 +25x\u00b2 -8x\u00b2 +40x -2x +16 -16=0\n\nSimplify:\n\n-x\u00b3 +17x\u00b2 +38x=0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\nx\u00b3 -17x\u00b2 -38x=0\n\nFactor out x:\n\nx(x\u00b2 -17x -38)=0\n\nSolutions: x=0 or x\u00b2 -17x -38=0\n\nSolve quadratic:\n\nx = [17 \u00b1sqrt(289 +152)]/2 = [17 \u00b1sqrt(441)]/2 = [17 \u00b121]/2\n\nThus,\n\nx=(17+21)/2=38/2=19\n\nx=(17-21)/2=-4/2=-2\n\nSo from case 1, y=4 +5x, we have x=0,19,-2.\n\nBut wait, x=0:\n\nThen y=4 +5*0=4. So (0,4). As before.\n\nx=19:\n\ny=4 +5*19=4 +95=99. As before.\n\nx=-2:\n\ny=4 +5*(-2)=4 -10=-6. So (x=-2, y=-6). Which is one of the solutions we found.\n\nBut wait, earlier we had x=-2 with y=\u00b16. Here, x=-2 gives y=-6. So where is y=6?\n\nAh, in case 2: y=4 -x. Let's check that.\n\nCase 2: y=4 -x\n\nPlug into first equation:\n\n(4 -x)^2 = (x +8)(x\u00b2 +2)\n\nLeft side:16 -8x +x\u00b2\n\nRight side:x\u00b3 +8x\u00b2 +2x +16\n\nSet equal:\n\nx\u00b2 -8x +16 =x\u00b3 +8x\u00b2 +2x +16\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n-x\u00b3 -7x\u00b2 -10x=0\n\nMultiply by -1:\n\nx\u00b3 +7x\u00b2 +10x=0\n\nFactor out x:\n\nx(x\u00b2 +7x +10)=0\n\nSo x=0 or x\u00b2 +7x +10=0\n\nSolve quadratic:\n\nx= [-7 \u00b1sqrt(49 -40)]/2 = [-7 \u00b13]/2\n\nThus, x=(-7 +3)/2=-4/2=-2; x=(-7 -3)/2=-10/2=-5\n\nSo from case 2: y=4 -x, we get x=0,-2,-5.\n\nx=0:\n\ny=4 -0=4. Which is the same as in case 1.\n\nx=-2:\n\ny=4 -(-2)=6. So (x=-2,y=6). Which was the other solution for x=-2.\n\nx=-5:\n\ny=4 -(-5)=9. So (x=-5,y=9). As before.\n\nSo combining both cases:\n\nFrom case1: x=0,y=4; x=19,y=99; x=-2,y=-6\n\nFrom case2: x=0,y=4; x=-2,y=6; x=-5,y=9\n\nTherefore, unique solutions are:\n\n(x=-2,y=-6), (x=-2,y=6), (x=-5,y=9), (x=0,y=4), (x=19,y=99)\n\nThus, the same as before. So all solutions are valid.\n\nTherefore, the system has five solutions.\n\nI think this confirms all the solutions. So the final answer should list all these points.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe solutions to the system are \\(\\boxed{(-2, 6)}\\), \\(\\boxed{(-2, -6)}\\), \\(\\boxed{(-5, 9)}\\), \\(\\boxed{(0, 4)}\\), and \\(\\boxed{(19, 99)}\\).", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the system of equations:\n\\[ y^2 = (x+8)(x^2 + 2) \\]\n\\[ y^2 - (8+4x)y + (16+16x-5x^2) = 0 \\]\n\nwe start by substituting \\( y^2 \\) from the first equation into the second equation. This gives:\n\\[ (x + 8)(x^2 + 2) - (8 + 4x)y + (16 + 16x - 5x^2) = 0 \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying, we find:\n\\[ x^3 + 3x^2 + 18x + 32 - (8 + 4x)y = 0 \\]\nSolving for \\( y \\), we get:\n\\[ y = \\frac{x^3 + 3x^2 + 18x + 32}{8 + 4x} \\]\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we find:\n\\[ y = \\frac{(x + 2)(x^2 + x + 16)}{4(x + 2)} \\]\nThis simplifies to \\( y = \\frac{x^2 + x + 16}{4} \\) when \\( x \\neq -2 \\). For \\( x = -2 \\), we check the original equations and find \\( y = \\pm 6 \\).\n\nNext, we substitute \\( y = \\frac{x^2 + x + 16}{4} \\) into the first equation and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{x^2 + x + 16}{4} \\right)^2 = (x + 8)(x^2 + 2) \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ x^4 - 14x^3 - 95x^2 = 0 \\]\nFactoring, we get:\n\\[ x^2(x^2 - 14x - 95) = 0 \\]\nSolving the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - 14x - 95 = 0 \\), we find \\( x = 19 \\) and \\( x = -5 \\). Additionally, \\( x = 0 \\) is a root.\n\nWe also solve the second equation using the quadratic formula for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = \\frac{(8 + 4x) \\pm 6|x|}{2} \\]\nThis gives two cases for \\( y \\): \\( y = 4 + 5x \\) and \\( y = 4 - x \\). Substituting these into the first equation, we find the solutions for \\( x \\) and verify all solutions.\n\nThe solutions to the system are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 6)}, \\boxed{(-2, -6)}, \\boxed{(-5, 9)}, \\boxed{(0, 4)}, \\boxed{(19, 99)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the equations:\n\n\\[\ny^2 = (x+8)(x^2 + 2)\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\ny^2 - (8 + 4x)y + (16 + 16x - 5x^2) = 0.\n\\]\n\n1. **Understand the second equation:**\n\n   The second equation is a quadratic in \\( y \\):\n\n   \\[\n   y^2 - (8 + 4x)y + (16 + 16x - 5x^2) = 0.\n   \\]\n\n   We denote the coefficients of the quadratic as follows:\n   \n   \\[\n   a = 1, \\quad b = -(8 + 4x), \\quad c = 16 + 16x - 5x^2.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve the quadratic equation for \\( y \\):**\n\n   Using the quadratic formula \\( y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), we substitute \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\):\n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{ -(8 + 4x) \\pm \\sqrt{(8 + 4x)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (16 + 16x - 5x^2)} }{2 \\cdot 1}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying inside the square root:\n\n   \\[\n   (8 + 4x)^2 = 64 + 64x + 16x^2\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   4ac = 4 \\cdot (16 + 16x - 5x^2) = 64 + 64x - 20x^2\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{(64 + 64x + 16x^2) - (64 + 64x - 20x^2)} = \\sqrt{16x^2 + 20x^2} = \\sqrt{36x^2} = 6x\n   \\]\n\n   Thus,\n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{ -(8 + 4x) \\pm 6x }{2}\n   \\]\n\n   This gives us two possible values for \\( y \\):\n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{-8 - 4x + 6x}{2} = \\frac{-8 + 2x}{2} = -4 + x\n   \\]\n\n   and\n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{-8 - 4x - 6x}{2} = \\frac{-8 - 10x}{2} = -4 - 5x\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute \\( y \\) into the first equation:**\n\n   Recall the first equation:\n\n   \\[\n   y^2 = (x+8)(x^2 + 2)\n   \\]\n\n   Let's test each value of \\( y \\):\n\n   Substituting \\( y = -4 + x \\):\n\n   \\[\n   (-4 + x)^2 = (x + 8)(x^2 + 2)\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   (x - 4)^2 = (x + 8)(x^2 + 2)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 8x + 16 = x^3 + 8x + 2x^2 + 16\n   \\]\n\n   On simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   x^3 + x^2 + 16x + 16 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Factorizing this polynomial:\n\n   We find roots as \\( x = 0 \\), \\(x = -2 \\) (by trial and error), and factorizing further we get:\n\n   Removing factor \\( x+2 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   x^3 + 10x^2 + 40x + 80 - 20x^2 + 80x = 0 \n   \\]\n\n   Factoring:\n\n   \\[\n   ( x + 0 ) ( x + 5 ) ( x - 19) )\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find the corresponding \\( y \\):**\n\n   For \\( x = 0 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   y^2 = 8 \\times 2 \\rightarrow y^2 = 16 \\rightarrow y = \\pm 4\n   \\]\n\n   For \\( x = -2 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   y^2 = (-2 + 8)((-2)^2 + 2) \\rightarrow y^2 = 6 \\times 6 \\rightarrow y = \\pm 6\n   \\]\n\n   For \\( x = -5 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   y^2 = (-5 + 8)((-5)^2 + 2) \\rightarrow y^2 = 3 \\times 27 \\rightarrow y = \\pm 9\n   \\]\n\n   For \\( x = 19 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   y^2 = (19 + 8)((19)^2 + 2) \\rightarrow  y^2 = 27( 361 + 2 ) \\rightarrow y = \\pm 99\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ ( x = 0, y = -4 \\pm 4 ) ( x = -2, y = \\pm 6 ) ( x = 19 , y 99 ) } \n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the integral \\(\\int \\frac{x^{4}-2 x^{3}+3 x^{2}}{x^{2}} \\, dx\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the integral of (x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2) divided by x\u00b2 with respect to x. Hmm, let me start by understanding what this integral is asking. The integrand is a rational function, right? A polynomial divided by another polynomial. Maybe I can simplify this before trying to integrate term by term. \n\nFirst, let me write down the integral again to make sure I have it right: \u222b(x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2)/x\u00b2 dx. Yeah, that's the one. So, the numerator is x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2 and the denominator is x\u00b2. Since all terms in the numerator have x squared as a factor, perhaps I can divide each term by x\u00b2 individually. That might simplify things.\n\nLet me try that. So, breaking it down term by term:\n\n- The first term is x\u2074 divided by x\u00b2. Using the laws of exponents, x\u2074 / x\u00b2 = x^(4-2) = x\u00b2. Okay, that works.\n- The second term is -2x\u00b3 divided by x\u00b2. Similarly, x\u00b3 / x\u00b2 = x^(3-2) = x, so that term becomes -2x.\n- The third term is 3x\u00b2 divided by x\u00b2. That would be 3x^(2-2) = 3x^0 = 3*1 = 3.\n\nSo putting it all together, the integrand simplifies to x\u00b2 - 2x + 3. That's much easier to handle. Now, instead of integrating the original complicated fraction, I can integrate this polynomial term by term.\n\nAlright, so the integral becomes \u222b(x\u00b2 - 2x + 3) dx. Let me remember how to integrate each term. The integral of x^n dx is (x^(n+1))/(n+1) + C, as long as n \u2260 -1. So here, each term is a power of x, so we can apply this rule.\n\nLet's tackle each term one by one:\n\n1. The integral of x\u00b2 dx: Here, n = 2, so it becomes x^(2+1)/(2+1) = x\u00b3/3.\n2. The integral of -2x dx: First, factor out the constant -2. The integral of x dx is x\u00b2/2, so multiplying by -2 gives -2*(x\u00b2/2) = -x\u00b2.\n3. The integral of 3 dx: The integral of a constant is the constant times x. So 3 integrates to 3x.\n\nPutting all these together, we have:\n\n\u222b(x\u00b2 - 2x + 3) dx = (x\u00b3/3) - x\u00b2 + 3x + C, where C is the constant of integration.\n\nWait, let me check if I did each step correctly. For the first term, x\u00b2 integrates to x\u00b3/3 \u2013 that seems right. The second term, integrating -2x, yes, the integral of x is x\u00b2/2, times -2 gives -x\u00b2. Third term, integrating 3 gives 3x. Looks good. And of course, we can't forget the constant C at the end because it's an indefinite integral.\n\nSo, combining everything, the solution should be (x\u00b3)/3 - x\u00b2 + 3x + C. Let me just verify by differentiating this result to see if we get back the original integrand.\n\nDifferentiating term by term:\n\n- The derivative of x\u00b3/3 is 3x\u00b2/3 = x\u00b2.\n- The derivative of -x\u00b2 is -2x.\n- The derivative of 3x is 3.\n- The derivative of C is 0.\n\nAdding those up: x\u00b2 - 2x + 3, which is exactly the simplified integrand we had after dividing by x\u00b2. So that checks out. Therefore, integrating that gives the correct antiderivative.\n\nJust to recap the steps: I first simplified the original fraction by dividing each term in the numerator by the denominator, x\u00b2. This made the integral much simpler, converting it into a polynomial which is straightforward to integrate term by term. After integrating each term, I combined them and added the constant of integration. Then, as a check, I differentiated the result to ensure it matches the simplified integrand, which it does. So, the answer should be correct.\n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. Let me just consider if there's another way to approach this. For example, maybe using substitution? But substitution seems unnecessary here because the integrand simplifies so nicely. If I didn't simplify first, could I still integrate directly? Let's see. The original integrand is (x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2)/x\u00b2. If I didn't split the numerator, perhaps I could factor out x\u00b2 from the numerator first. Let's see:\n\nx\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2 = x\u00b2(x\u00b2 - 2x + 3). Then, dividing by x\u00b2 gives x\u00b2 - 2x + 3. So that's essentially the same step as before. Therefore, simplifying the expression first is the key step here.\n\nAlternatively, could I use polynomial long division? But since the denominator is a monomial (x\u00b2), dividing each term individually is sufficient and quicker than setting up long division. So that method works fine.\n\nAnother check: suppose I didn't simplify first and tried to integrate term by term in the original fraction. Let's see what would happen. The original integrand is (x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2)/x\u00b2. If I split the integral into three separate fractions:\n\n\u222b(x\u2074/x\u00b2) dx - \u222b(2x\u00b3/x\u00b2) dx + \u222b(3x\u00b2/x\u00b2) dx.\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\u222bx\u00b2 dx - 2\u222bx dx + 3\u222b1 dx.\n\nWhich is exactly the same as integrating x\u00b2 - 2x + 3. So, whether I simplify first or split the integral first, I end up with the same terms. So both approaches are valid and lead to the same result.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the method, the integral simplifies to (x\u00b3)/3 - x\u00b2 + 3x + C.\n\nJust to make sure there's no calculation error, let me go through the coefficients again. For the x\u00b2 term, integrating gives x\u00b3/3. The coefficient is 1, so that's correct. The second term was -2x, integrating that gives -2*(x\u00b2/2) = -x\u00b2. Correct. The third term, 3, integrates to 3x. Yes. So the coefficients all check out.\n\nI think that's thorough enough. The key insight was simplifying the integrand before integrating, which made the problem straightforward. If someone tried to integrate without simplifying, they might make mistakes or overcomplicate things. But here, breaking it down term by term first was the right approach.\n\nAnother thing to note is that when dividing by x\u00b2, we have to ensure that x \u2260 0 to avoid division by zero. However, since this is an indefinite integral, we typically consider the domain where the integrand is defined, which in this case is all real numbers except x = 0. But since the original integrand is undefined at x = 0, the antiderivative will also have restrictions on its domain. However, in the context of standard calculus problems, unless specified, we usually proceed without worrying about the domain unless there's a specific reason. But it's good to be aware that x = 0 is excluded from the domain here.\n\nBut given that the problem didn't specify any particular domain, and since the antiderivative we found is a polynomial (except for the original division by x\u00b2), the expression (x\u00b3)/3 - x\u00b2 + 3x + C is valid wherever the original integrand is defined, i.e., x \u2260 0. However, the antiderivative itself, being a polynomial, is defined for all real numbers. There's a slight nuance here. The original function (x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2)/x\u00b2 is equivalent to x\u00b2 - 2x + 3 for all x \u2260 0, so the antiderivative we found is valid for x \u2260 0. But technically, the expression (x\u00b3)/3 - x\u00b2 + 3x + C is a valid antiderivative for x\u00b2 - 2x + 3 on any interval not containing x = 0. Since x\u00b2 - 2x + 3 is defined everywhere, but the original integrand was only defined for x \u2260 0. Wait, this seems a bit conflicting.\n\nWait a second, x\u00b2 - 2x + 3 is a polynomial and is defined for all real numbers. However, the original integrand was (x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2)/x\u00b2. If we factor x\u00b2 from the numerator, we get x\u00b2(x\u00b2 - 2x + 3)/x\u00b2 = x\u00b2 - 2x + 3, but this cancellation is only valid when x\u00b2 \u2260 0, i.e., x \u2260 0. Therefore, the original integrand is actually equal to x\u00b2 - 2x + 3 for all x except x = 0. However, when we integrate, the function x\u00b2 - 2x + 3 is continuous everywhere, including at x = 0. Therefore, the integral of the original function (which is undefined at x = 0) would actually be the integral of x\u00b2 - 2x + 3, but with a note that the original function wasn't defined at x = 0.\n\nBut in standard practice, when simplifying the integrand by canceling x\u00b2, we have to consider that x \u2260 0. However, the resulting antiderivative is a polynomial, which is defined for all real numbers. So, the integral is correct, but strictly speaking, the antiderivative is valid on any interval that doesn't include x = 0. But since the original function has a removable discontinuity at x = 0, some might consider the antiderivative as valid for all real numbers by continuous extension. But technically, the integral is valid on intervals where x \u2260 0. However, in most calculus problems, especially in basic ones, this point is often glossed over unless specifically required.\n\nIn any case, since the problem just asks for the integral, and we've followed proper simplification steps, the answer (x\u00b3)/3 - x\u00b2 + 3x + C is correct. The constant C would account for any constant of integration, and the solution is valid on any interval not containing x = 0. But again, unless the problem specifies, we usually present the antiderivative as is.\n\nTherefore, after careful consideration and multiple checks, I'm confident that the integral \u222b(x\u2074 - 2x\u00b3 + 3x\u00b2)/x\u00b2 dx is equal to (x\u00b3)/3 - x\u00b2 + 3x + C.", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the integral \\(\\int \\frac{x^{4}-2 x^{3}+3 x^{2}}{x^{2}} \\, dx\\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Simplify the Integrand**:  \n   Divide each term in the numerator by \\(x^2\\):  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^4}{x^2} - \\frac{2x^3}{x^2} + \\frac{3x^2}{x^2} = x^2 - 2x + 3.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Integrate Term by Term**:  \n   Now integrate the simplified polynomial:  \n   \\[\n   \\int (x^2 - 2x + 3) \\, dx.\n   \\]\n   - \\(\\int x^2 \\, dx = \\frac{x^3}{3}\\),  \n   - \\(\\int -2x \\, dx = -x^2\\),  \n   - \\(\\int 3 \\, dx = 3x\\).\n\n3. **Combine Results**:  \n   Sum the integrals and add the constant of integration \\(C\\):  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^3}{3} - x^2 + 3x + C.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verification**:  \n   Differentiate the result to confirm it matches the simplified integrand:  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{x^3}{3} - x^2 + 3x + C\\right) = x^2 - 2x + 3.\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - x^{2} + 3x + C}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Rewrite the integrand by simplifying the fraction**:\n   \\[\n   \\int \\frac{x^{4} - 2x^{3} + 3x^{2}}{x^{2}} \\, dx\n   \\]\n   Divide each term in the numerator by the denominator \\(x^2\\):\n   \\[\n   \\int \\left( \\frac{x^4}{x^2} - \\frac{2x^3}{x^2} + \\frac{3x^2}{x^2} \\right) \\, dx = \\int \\left( x^2 - 2x + 3 \\right) \\, dx\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express the integral as a sum of separate integrals**:\n   \\[\n   \\int \\left( x^2 - 2x + 3 \\right) \\, dx = \\int x^2 \\, dx - 2 \\int x \\, dx + 3 \\int 1 \\, dx\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find each integral separately**:\n   - For \\(\\int x^2 \\, dx\\), use the power rule of integration \\(\\int x^n \\, dx = \\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C\\):\n     \\[\n     \\int x^2 \\, dx = \\frac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} = \\frac{x^3}{3}\n     \\]\n   - For \\(\\int x \\, dx\\), again use the power rule:\n     \\[\n     \\int x \\, dx = \\frac{x^{1+1}}{1+1} = \\frac{x^2}{2}\n     \\]\n   - For \\(\\int 1 \\, dx\\), this is a constant function's integral:\n     \\[\n     \\int 1 \\, dx = x\n     \\]\n\n4. **Substitute these results back into the expression**:\n   \\[\n   \\int x^2 \\, dx - 2 \\int x \\, dx + 3 \\int 1 \\, dx = \\frac{x^3}{3} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{x^2}{2} + 3x\n   \\]\n   Simplify the constants:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^3}{3} - x^2 + 3x\n   \\]\n\n5. **Add the constant of integration \\(C\\)**:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^3}{3} - x^2 + 3x + C\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{x^3}{3} - x^2 + 3x + C}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In the pyramid \\(A B C D\\), the dihedral angles with the edges \\(A B\\), \\(B C\\), and \\(C A\\) are \\(\\alpha_1\\), \\(\\alpha_2\\), and \\(\\alpha_3\\), respectively, and the areas of triangles \\(A B D\\), \\(B C D\\), and \\(C A D\\) are \\(S_1\\), \\(S_2\\), and \\(S_3\\), respectively. The area of triangle \\(A B C\\) is \\(S\\). Prove that \\(S = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3\\) (some of the angles \\(\\alpha_1\\), \\(\\alpha_2\\), and \\(\\alpha_3\\) may be obtuse).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the area S of triangle ABC is equal to the sum of the areas of the three adjacent triangles ABD, BCD, and CAD, each multiplied by the cosine of their respective dihedral angles \u03b1\u2081, \u03b1\u2082, \u03b1\u2083. Hmm, dihedral angles... right, those are the angles between two planes. So in a pyramid ABCD, the dihedral angles along edges AB, BC, and CA would be the angles between the faces that meet along those edges.\n\nLet me visualize this pyramid. The base is triangle ABC, and the apex is D. The three triangular faces are ABD, BCD, and CAD. Each of these faces forms a dihedral angle with the base ABC along the edges AB, BC, and CA respectively. The dihedral angles are \u03b1\u2081, \u03b1\u2082, \u03b1\u2083. The areas of these three triangular faces are S\u2081, S\u2082, S\u2083. The problem states that S = S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081 + S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 + S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083. I need to show this relationship holds.\n\nFirst, let me recall that the dihedral angle between two planes can be related to the angle between their normals. Also, when projecting a face onto another plane, the area of the projection is the original area multiplied by the cosine of the angle between the planes. That might be useful here. Since S is the area of the base ABC, perhaps each of the adjacent faces ABD, BCD, CAD, when projected onto the base ABC, contribute an area equal to their original area times the cosine of the dihedral angle. Then, the sum of these projections would equal the area of ABC. That seems plausible. Let me check.\n\nIf we consider each lateral face (ABD, BCD, CAD) and project them onto the base ABC, the projected area of each would be S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081, S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082, S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083. If these projections cover the entire base ABC without overlapping, then their sum would be equal to S. But I need to verify if that's the case. However, in a pyramid, each lateral face projects onto the base, but depending on the shape of the pyramid, these projections might overlap or not. Wait, but in a convex pyramid, the projections of the lateral faces should exactly cover the base. So maybe the total area of the projections is equal to the base area. That makes sense. So each lateral face's projection contributes a part of the base, and since the pyramid is convex, their sum covers the entire base. Therefore, the sum of the projected areas equals the base area.\n\nBut let me think more carefully. Let's take one face, say ABD. The dihedral angle along AB is \u03b1\u2081. When we project triangle ABD onto the base ABC, the area becomes S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081. Similarly for the other two faces. If these three projections exactly cover ABC without overlapping, then S = S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081 + S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 + S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083. But I need to ensure that the projections indeed sum up to S. Is there a theorem that states this?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can approach this by considering the normals of the faces. Let\u2019s denote the base ABC as lying in the plane z = 0, and the apex D at some point (x, y, z). The dihedral angles between each lateral face and the base can be related to the angle between the normals of the respective faces.\n\nFor each lateral face, say ABD, its normal vector makes an angle \u03b1\u2081 with the normal of the base ABC. Since the base is in the z = 0 plane, its normal is (0, 0, 1). The normal vector of face ABD can be calculated using the cross product of vectors AB and AD. Let's denote vectors AB and AD as vectors in 3D space. The angle between the normal of ABD and the normal of ABC is the dihedral angle \u03b1\u2081. The area of the projection of ABD onto ABC is indeed S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081, which is a standard result from vector calculus: the area of the projection of a surface onto another plane is the original area times the cosine of the angle between the normals.\n\nTherefore, each lateral face's projection onto the base contributes S_i cos \u03b1_i, and since the projections cover the entire base (as the pyramid is a single convex shape), the sum of these projections must equal the area of the base. Hence, S = S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081 + S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 + S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083. That seems to make sense. But I should verify this with a more concrete approach, perhaps using coordinates or vector algebra.\n\nLet me try setting up coordinates. Let\u2019s place triangle ABC in the xy-plane. Let\u2019s assign coordinates to points A, B, C. For simplicity, let me take point A at (0, 0, 0), B at (b, 0, 0), and C at (c, d, 0). The apex D is at some point (p, q, h), where h is the height from D to the base ABC. However, in this problem, the dihedral angles are given, which relate to the angles between the faces. So perhaps instead of coordinates, it's better to use vectors and normals.\n\nFor each edge, say AB, the dihedral angle \u03b1\u2081 is the angle between the two faces ABD and ABC. To compute this dihedral angle, we can find the angle between the normals of these two faces. Let\u2019s compute the normal vectors.\n\nThe normal vector to the base ABC is straightforward since it's in the xy-plane; it's (0, 0, 1). For the face ABD, we can compute its normal vector by taking the cross product of vectors AB and AD. Let\u2019s denote vectors:\n\nVector AB = B - A = (b, 0, 0)\nVector AD = D - A = (p, q, h)\n\nCross product AB \u00d7 AD = |i \u00a0\u00a0j \u00a0\u00a0k|\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 |b \u00a0\u00a00 \u00a0\u00a00|\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 |p \u00a0\u00a0q \u00a0\u00a0h|\n\n= i*(0* h - 0*q) - j*(b*h - 0*p) + k*(b*q - 0*p)\n= 0i - (b h)j + (b q)k\n= (0, -b h, b q)\n\nSo the normal vector for face ABD is (0, -b h, b q). To find the dihedral angle \u03b1\u2081 between face ABD and ABC, we need the angle between this normal vector and the normal vector of ABC, which is (0, 0, 1). The cosine of the dihedral angle \u03b1\u2081 is the dot product of the unit normals divided by the product of their magnitudes.\n\nFirst, normalize the normal vector of ABD:\n\n|N_ABD| = sqrt(0\u00b2 + (-b h)\u00b2 + (b q)^2) = b sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)\n\nUnit normal of ABD: (0, -b h, b q) / (b sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)) = (0, -h, q)/sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)\n\nUnit normal of ABC: (0, 0, 1)\n\nDot product: (0, -h, q)/sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2) \u2022 (0, 0, 1) = q / sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)\n\nThus, cos \u03b1\u2081 = q / sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)\n\nSimilarly, we can compute S\u2081, the area of triangle ABD. The area is half the magnitude of the cross product AB \u00d7 AD.\n\n|AB \u00d7 AD| = sqrt(0\u00b2 + (b h)^2 + (b q)^2) = b sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)\n\nThus, S\u2081 = (1/2) * b sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)\n\nTherefore, S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081 = (1/2) * b sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2) * (q / sqrt(h\u00b2 + q\u00b2)) ) = (1/2) * b q\n\nWait, that's interesting. So S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081 simplifies to (1/2) * b q. Hmm, let me note that.\n\nSimilarly, let's compute S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 and S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083.\n\nFor edge BC, dihedral angle \u03b1\u2082. Let's compute the normal vector for face BCD.\n\nPoints B, C, D. Vector BC = C - B = (c - b, d, 0)\nVector BD = D - B = (p - b, q - 0, h - 0) = (p - b, q, h)\n\nCross product BC \u00d7 BD:\n\n|i \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 j \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 k|\n|c - b \u00a0 d \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 0|\n|p - b \u00a0 q \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 h|\n\n= i*(d*h - 0*q) - j*((c - b)h - 0*(p - b)) + k*((c - b)q - d(p - b))\n\n= (d h, - (c - b) h, (c - b) q - d (p - b)) \n\nSo the normal vector is (d h, - (c - b) h, (c - b) q - d (p - b))\n\nTo find the dihedral angle \u03b1\u2082 between face BCD and ABC, we take the dot product of the unit normal of BCD and (0, 0, 1).\n\nBut first, let's compute the unit normal of BCD.\n\nThe magnitude of BC \u00d7 BD is |N_BCD| = sqrt[(d h)^2 + (- (c - b) h)^2 + ((c - b) q - d (p - b))^2]\n\nThis seems complicated. Let's denote component-wise:\n\nN_BCD_x = d h\n\nN_BCD_y = - (c - b) h\n\nN_BCD_z = (c - b) q - d (p - b)\n\nDot product with (0, 0, 1) is N_BCD_z component. Therefore,\n\ncos \u03b1\u2082 = N_BCD_z / |N_BCD| = [ (c - b) q - d (p - b) ] / |N_BCD|\n\nSimilarly, the area S\u2082 is (1/2) |N_BCD|, so S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 = (1/2) |N_BCD| * [ (c - b) q - d (p - b) ] / |N_BCD| ) = (1/2) [ (c - b) q - d (p - b) ]\n\nSimilarly, for face CAD, edge CA, dihedral angle \u03b1\u2083. Let's compute S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083.\n\nPoints C, A, D. Vector CA = A - C = (-c, -d, 0)\nVector CD = D - C = (p - c, q - d, h - 0) = (p - c, q - d, h)\n\nCross product CA \u00d7 CD:\n\n|i \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 j \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 k|\n|-c \u00a0 \u00a0 -d \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 0|\n|p - c \u00a0 q - d \u00a0 h|\n\n= i*(-d * h - 0*(q - d)) - j*(-c * h - 0*(p - c)) + k*(-c*(q - d) - (-d)(p - c))\n\n= (-d h, c h, -c q + c d + d p - c d )\n\nSimplify the z-component:\n\n- c q + c d + d p - c d = -c q + d p\n\nTherefore, normal vector N_CAD = (-d h, c h, d p - c q )\n\nThe magnitude |N_CAD| = sqrt[ ( -d h )\u00b2 + ( c h )\u00b2 + ( d p - c q )\u00b2 ]\n\nThe dihedral angle \u03b1\u2083's cosine is the z-component of the unit normal:\n\ncos \u03b1\u2083 = (d p - c q) / |N_CAD|\n\nThen S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083 = (1/2) |N_CAD| * (d p - c q)/ |N_CAD| = (1/2)(d p - c q)\n\nNow, summing up S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081 + S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 + S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083:\n\n= (1/2) b q + (1/2)[ (c - b) q - d (p - b) ] + (1/2)(d p - c q )\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: (1/2) b q\n\nSecond term: (1/2)[ (c - b) q - d p + d b ]\n\nThird term: (1/2)(d p - c q )\n\nAdding all three:\n\n= (1/2) b q + (1/2)(c q - b q - d p + d b) + (1/2)(d p - c q )\n\nCombine terms:\n\n= [ (1/2) b q + (1/2)(c q - b q) ] + [ (1/2)( - d p ) + (1/2)(d p ) ] + (1/2 d b )\n\nSimplify each bracket:\n\nFirst bracket: (1/2 b q + 1/2 c q - 1/2 b q ) = 1/2 c q\n\nSecond bracket: ( -1/2 d p + 1/2 d p ) = 0\n\nThird term: 1/2 d b\n\nSo total sum: 1/2 c q + 1/2 d b\n\nBut the area S of triangle ABC is (1/2) | (B - A) \u00d7 (C - A) | \n\nVectors AB = (b, 0, 0) and AC = (c, d, 0). Cross product AB \u00d7 AC = (0, 0, b d - 0 c ) = (0, 0, b d ). Thus, the area S is (1/2) | b d | = (1/2) b d.\n\nBut according to our sum, we have 1/2 c q + 1/2 d b. Wait, that's (1/2)(c q + d b). But S is (1/2) b d. Unless c q + d b = b d. How does that hold?\n\nWait, in our coordinate system, point D is (p, q, h). But in the pyramid, the apex D is connected to all three vertices A, B, C. So the coordinates of D must satisfy the condition that D lies above the base ABC. However, unless there's a specific relation between p, q, c, d, etc., we can't assume c q + d b = b d. This suggests that my approach might have a mistake, or perhaps I made a wrong assumption in coordinate assignments.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check the cross product calculation again. For the normal vectors and the areas, perhaps I missed something. Let me retrace.\n\nFirst, when I calculated S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081, I got (1/2) b q. Similarly, S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 was (1/2)[ (c - b) q - d (p - b) ], and S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083 was (1/2)(d p - c q ). Adding these together gives (1/2)(b q + c q - b q - d p + d b + d p - c q ) = (1/2)(d b). Which is indeed equal to (1/2) b d, the area S. Wait, let me recheck:\n\nFirst term: (1/2) b q\n\nSecond term: (1/2)[ (c - b) q - d (p - b) ] = (1/2)(c q - b q - d p + d b )\n\nThird term: (1/2)(d p - c q )\n\nAdding all three:\n\n(1/2 b q) + (1/2 c q - 1/2 b q - 1/2 d p + 1/2 d b ) + (1/2 d p - 1/2 c q )\n\nNow, combining like terms:\n\n1/2 b q - 1/2 b q = 0\n\n1/2 c q - 1/2 c q = 0\n\n-1/2 d p + 1/2 d p = 0\n\nRemaining term: 1/2 d b\n\nSo indeed, the sum is (1/2) d b, which is exactly the area S of triangle ABC. Therefore, S = S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081 + S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082 + S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083. \n\nTherefore, the equation holds. Wait, so through coordinate analysis, assigning coordinates to the pyramid, and computing each term, the sum indeed equals the area S. This confirms the formula.\n\nBut this was a coordinate-based proof. The problem is general, not assuming any specific coordinates. So does this hold for any pyramid? Since we chose arbitrary coordinates (except placing ABC in the xy-plane), the result should hold generally. However, the apex D was assigned coordinates (p, q, h), and through the computation, the terms canceled out to give S = (1/2) b d, which is indeed the area of ABC in this coordinate system. Therefore, regardless of where D is located (as long as the pyramid is non-degenerate), the relationship holds.\n\nTherefore, this proves the formula. The key idea is that when projecting each lateral face onto the base, the area of the projection is the original area times the cosine of the dihedral angle, and the sum of these projections equals the base area. The coordinate computation confirmed that the cross terms cancel out, leaving exactly the base area.\n\nAlternatively, there's a more geometric way to see this. For each lateral face, the projection onto the base ABC effectively \"flattens\" the face onto the base, scaling its area by the cosine of the dihedral angle. Since the three projections cover the entire base (as the pyramid is convex), the sum of these scaled areas must equal the base's area. This is analogous to how, in 3D, the area of a surface can be found by projecting it onto coordinate planes and summing appropriately, considering the angles.\n\nAnother approach could be using vectors and the concept of area vectors. Each triangular face has an area vector perpendicular to the face with magnitude equal to the area. The projection of these area vectors onto the base's normal direction (i.e., the z-axis in our coordinate system) would give contributions S\u2081 cos \u03b1\u2081, S\u2082 cos \u03b1\u2082, S\u2083 cos \u03b1\u2083. Since the pyramid is a closed surface, the sum of the projections of all area vectors onto any plane should relate to the enclosed volume. However, in this case, we are dealing with the base area, so perhaps the relation is simpler.\n\nWait, actually, considering the base itself has an area vector S in the direction of the normal (0,0,1). The sum of the projections of the lateral faces' area vectors onto the base's normal should equal the base's area. But since the base is part of the closed surface (the pyramid), the total flux through the pyramid would be zero, but here we are only considering the lateral faces and projecting them. Hmm, maybe not the best approach.\n\nAlternatively, think of the pyramid as three tetrahedrons each formed by the apex D and each edge of the base. But I think the projection idea is more straightforward.\n\nIn summary, the core idea is projecting each lateral face onto the base, calculating their areas multiplied by the cosine of the dihedral angles, and realizing that these projections sum up to the base area. The coordinate-based calculation confirmed this algebraically, showing that all variable terms cancel out, leaving the base area. Therefore, the formula holds true.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that the area \\( S \\) of triangle \\( ABC \\) is equal to \\( S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3 \\), we utilize the concept of projecting the lateral faces of the pyramid onto the base. Here's the step-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **Understanding Dihedral Angles**: The dihedral angle between two planes (faces) is the angle between their normals. For edge \\( AB \\), the dihedral angle \\( \\alpha_1 \\) is formed by the normals of faces \\( ABD \\) and \\( ABC \\). Similarly for \\( \\alpha_2 \\) and \\( \\alpha_3 \\).\n\n2. **Projection of Areas**: When a face is projected onto another plane, the projected area \\( S_{\\text{proj}} \\) relates to the original area \\( S_{\\text{original}} \\) by:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{proj}} = S_{\\text{original}} \\cos \\theta\n   \\]\n   where \\( \\theta \\) is the angle between the normals of the two planes.\n\n3. **Applying Projection to Each Face**:\n   - **Face \\( ABD \\)**: Projecting \\( ABD \\) onto \\( ABC \\) yields \\( S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 \\).\n   - **Face \\( BCD \\)**: Projecting \\( BCD \\) onto \\( ABC \\) yields \\( S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 \\).\n   - **Face \\( CAD \\)**: Projecting \\( CAD \\) onto \\( ABC \\) yields \\( S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3 \\).\n\n4. **Summing Projections**: Since the pyramid is convex, the projections of the three lateral faces exactly cover the base \\( ABC \\) without overlap. Thus, the sum of the projected areas equals the area of \\( ABC \\):\n   \\[\n   S = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3\n   \\]\n\n5. **Coordinate Verification**:\n   - Place \\( ABC \\) in the \\( xy \\)-plane with coordinates \\( A(0,0,0) \\), \\( B(b,0,0) \\), \\( C(c,d,0) \\), and apex \\( D(p,q,h) \\).\n   - Compute normals for each lateral face using cross products.\n   - Calculate \\( \\cos \\alpha_i \\) via dot products of normals.\n   - Show algebraically that \\( S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3 \\) simplifies to \\( \\frac{1}{2} | \\vec{AB} \\times \\vec{AC} | = S \\).\n\n**Conclusion**: The relationship \\( S = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3 \\) holds as the sum of the projected areas of the lateral faces onto the base equals the base area itself.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{S = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's denote $O$ as the base of the altitude \\( DO \\) of the pyramid \\( ABCD \\). The triangles \\( AOB \\), \\( AOC \\), and \\( BOC \\) are the orthogonal projections of the triangles \\( ADB \\), \\( ADC \\), and \\( BDC \\) respectively, onto the plane \\( ABC \\).\n\n1. **Case 1: Point \\( O \\) lies inside the triangle \\( ABC \\)**\n\n   If the base \\( O \\) of the altitude \\( DO \\) lies inside the triangle \\( ABC \\) (see Fig.1), then the dihedral angles \\( \\alpha_1 \\), \\( \\alpha_2 \\), and \\( \\alpha_3 \\) are all acute angles. Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\triangle AOB} = S_{\\triangle ADB} \\cos \\alpha_1 = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1\n   \\]\n   \n   Similarly, for the other projections:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\triangle AOC} = S_{\\triangle ADC} \\cos \\alpha_2 = S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_{\\triangle BOC} = S_{\\triangle BDC} \\cos \\alpha_3 = S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3\n   \\]\n\n   Summing these areas gives:\n   \\[\n   S = S_{\\triangle AOB} + S_{\\triangle AOC} + S_{\\triangle BOC} = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3\n   \\]\n   \n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   S = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case 2: Point \\( O \\) lies outside the triangle \\( ABC \\)**\n\n   If the base \\( O \\) of the altitude \\( DO \\) lies outside the triangle \\( ABC \\) (see Fig.2), then two of the angles \\( \\alpha_1 \\), \\( \\alpha_2 \\), and \\( \\alpha_3 \\) are obtuse. For instance, if \\( \\alpha_2 > 90^\\circ \\) and \\( \\alpha_3 > 90^\\circ \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n      S &= S_{\\triangle AOB} - S_{\\triangle AOC} - S_{\\triangle BOC} \\\\\n      &= S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 - S_2 \\cos(180^\\circ - \\alpha_2) - S_3 \\cos(180^\\circ - \\alpha_3) \\\\\n      &= S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3 \n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   This is due to the fact that \\( \\cos(180^\\circ - \\alpha) = -\\cos \\alpha \\).\n\nSimilarly, these results hold for the other cases where one or more of the angles are obtuse.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, we have proven that the area \\( S \\) of triangle \\( ABC \\) is given by:\n\\[\nS = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3\n\\]\n\\(\\boxed{S = S_1 \\cos \\alpha_1 + S_2 \\cos \\alpha_2 + S_3 \\cos \\alpha_3}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A certain number with a sum of digits equal to 2021 was divided by 7 and resulted in a number composed exclusively of the digit 7. How many digits 7 can this number contain? If there are multiple answers, provide their sum.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to figure out how many digits of 7 can a number have if when you divide it by 7, you get another number that's made up only of the digit 7. Also, the original number has a digit sum of 2021. If there are multiple possibilities, I have to sum them up. Hmm, let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's understand the problem. Let me restate it t