tion vector. So unless the translation vector is an eigenvector, but this gets complicated.\n\nWait, perhaps given that the transformation is linear, the only invariant lines are those through the origin. Let me check.\n\nSuppose a line L is given by ax + by = c. After transformation T, the image of a point (x, y) is (x', y') = (x + sqrt(3)y, sqrt(3)x - y). So to find L such that for all (x, y) on L, T(x, y) is also on L.\n\nSo substituting x' and y' into the equation of L: a x' + b y' = c.\n\nWhich is a(x + sqrt(3)y) + b(sqrt(3)x - y) = c.\n\nSimplify: (a + b sqrt(3))x + (a sqrt(3) - b)y = c.\n\nBut the original line is ax + by = c. Therefore, for all (x, y) satisfying ax + by = c, we must have (a + b sqrt(3))x + (a sqrt(3) - b)y = c.\n\nSo this implies that the two equations are scalar multiples of each other. That is, there exists a scalar \u03bb such that:\n\na + b sqrt(3) = \u03bb a\n\na sqrt(3) - b = \u03bb b\n\nand also, if c \u22600, then \u03bb c = c, so \u03bb =1. If c =0, then the line is through the origin, and we can have any \u03bb.\n\nTherefore, two cases:\n\nCase 1: c \u22600.\n\nThen we must have \u03bb=1. Therefore:\n\na + b sqrt(3) = a \u21d2 b sqrt(3) =0 \u21d2 b=0 (since sqrt(3) \u22600).\n\nSimilarly, a sqrt(3) - b = b \u21d2 a sqrt(3) = 2b. But if b=0, then a sqrt(3)=0 \u21d2 a=0. But a and b can't both be zero. Therefore, no solution in this case. Hence, there are no invariant lines with c \u22600.\n\nCase 2: c=0.\n\nThen the line is ax + by =0. Then we need:\n\n(a + b sqrt(3))x + (a sqrt(3) - b)y =0 must be the same line as ax + by=0. Therefore, there exists \u03bb \u22600 such that:\n\na + b sqrt(3) = \u03bb a\n\na sqrt(3) - b = \u03bb b\n\nSo:\n\n1) a + b sqrt(3) = \u03bb a \u21d2 b sqrt(3) = (\u03bb -1) a\n\n2) a sqrt(3) - b = \u03bb b \u21d2 a sqrt(3) = (\u03bb +1) b\n\nLet\u2019s solve for \u03bb. From equation 1: b sqrt(3) = (\u03bb -1) a \u21d2 a = [b sqrt(3)] / (\u03bb -1)\n\nFrom equation 2: a sqrt(3) = (\u03bb +1) b \u21d2 substitu