tances**:\n   - \\( AG = |m + 1| \\)\n   - \\( BF = \\left| \\frac{1}{m} - 1 \\right| = \\left| \\frac{1 - m}{m} \\right| \\)\n   - \\( AF = \\left| \\frac{1}{m} + 1 \\right| = \\left| \\frac{1 + m}{m} \\right| \\)\n   - \\( GB = |1 - m| \\)\n\n6. **Verifying the Product**:\n   - \\( AG \\cdot BF = |m + 1| \\cdot \\left| \\frac{1 - m}{m} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{(m + 1)(1 - m)}{m} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{1 - m^2}{m} \\right| \\)\n   - \\( AF \\cdot GB = \\left| \\frac{1 + m}{m} \\right| \\cdot |1 - m| = \\left| \\frac{(1 + m)(1 - m)}{m} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{1 - m^2}{m} \\right| \\)\n\nSince both products are equal, \\( AG \\cdot BF = AF \\cdot GB \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AG \\cdot BF = AF \\cdot GB}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the Problem Setup:**\n   - Circle \\(k\\) has diameters \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) that are perpendicular to each other.\n   - Line \\(e\\) passes through point \\(D\\) and intersects circle \\(k\\) at point \\(E\\), and intersects line \\(AB\\) at point \\(F\\).\n   - The intersection of lines \\(EC\\) and \\(AB\\) is point \\(G\\).\n   - We need to prove that \\(AG \\cdot BF = AF \\cdot GB\\).\n\n2. **Introducing Notations and Variables:**\n   - Let \\(O\\) be the center of circle \\(k\\) and \\(r\\) be its radius.\n   - Let point \\(G\\) be such that \\(GB = x\\) and \\(FB = y\\).\n\n3. **Distance Relations:**\n   - Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are diametrically opposite points, \\(AB = 2r\\).\n   - The lengths \\(AG\\) and \\(AF\\) can be written as:\n     \\[\n     AG = AB + BG = 2r + x,\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     AF = AB - BF = 2r - y.\n     \\]\n   - Our goal is to show:\n     \\[\n     (2r + x) y = (2r - y) x.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Using Similarity of Right Triangles:**\n   - Note that \\(\\triangle OGC\\) and \\(\\triangle ODF\\) are right triangles because \\(CD \\perp AB\\).\n   - Therefore, these triangles are similar by the AA criterion (angle-angle similarity) since both share a right angle, and \\(\\angle OGC = 