etermine the radical axis:**\n\n   The radical axis of two circles is the locus of points where the power of the point with respect to both circles is equal. This axis will be perpendicular to the line connecting the centers of the circles.\n\n   - The perpendicular from \\( A \\) to \\( BF \\) is the radical axis of \\( \\Gamma_B \\) and \\( \\Gamma_F \\). By the definition of the radical axis, the points on this line satisfy:\n     \\[\n     PB^2 - AB^2 = PF^2 - AF^2\n     \\]\n\n4. **Consider other perpendiculars similarly:**\n\n   - The perpendicular from \\( C \\) to \\( BD \\) is the radical axis of \\( \\Gamma_B \\) and \\( \\Gamma_D \\). The points on this line must satisfy:\n     \\[\n     PB^2 - AB^2 = PD^2 - DC^2\n     \\]\n\n   - Therefore, the intersection of these two radical axes satisfies:\n     \\[\n     PB^2 - AB^2 = PF^2 - AF^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad PB^2 - AB^2 = PD^2 - DC^2\n     \\]\n\n5. **Intersection condition:**\n\n   The point of intersection is common to the radical axes of \\( \\Gamma_B \\) and \\( \\Gamma_D \\), and \\( \\Gamma_B \\) and \\( \\Gamma_F \\). This intersection point thus satisfies:\n     \\[\n     PD^2 - DC^2 = PF^2 - AF^2\n     \\]\n\n6. **Concurrent point verification:**\n\n   Since this point satisfies the condition for perpendiculars from \\( E \\) to \\( DF \\), it must also lie on the radical axis of \\( \\Gamma_D \\) and \\( \\Gamma_F \\). Hence, the perpendicular from \\( E \\) to \\( DF \\) must pass through this same point.\n\n7. **Conclusion:**\n\n   Therefore, the perpendiculars from \\( A \\) to \\( FB \\), \\( C \\) to \\( BD \\), and \\( E \\) to \\( DF \\) are concurrent.\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{The perpendiculars from \\( A \\) to \\( FB \\), \\( C \\) to \\( BD \\), and \\( E \\) to \\( DF \\) are concurrent.}}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Each hotel room can accommodate no more than 3 people. The hotel manager knows t