um of white cells at any time.\n\nBut in the final grid, the sum of black cells equals the sum of white cells. The total sum is 5K, so since sum_black = sum_white, each must be 5K/2. Therefore, 5K must be even, which we already knew, so K must be even. But how does this help?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think about the number of times each cell is incremented or decremented. Each cell's value is equal to the number of times it was added minus the number of times it was subtracted. But since operations are on pairs, each addition or subtraction affects two cells. So the value in each cell is (number of times it was part of an addition) minus (number of times it was part of a subtraction).\n\nBut I need to relate this to the parity of the total number of operations. Let me denote the total number of operations as N = A + B, where A is the number of additions and B is the number of subtractions. We need to show that N is even.\n\nAnother idea: look at the parity of the entries in the grid. Since each operation affects two cells, each operation changes the parity of two cells. Starting from all even (zero), after each operation, two cells become odd. Then, the next operation could flip two cells again, etc. However, the final grid has all row sums equal and column sums equal. If all row sums are equal, then each row sum K must be even, as we saw before. So each row sum is even, which means that in each row, the number of odd entries must be even (since the sum of an even number of odd numbers is even, and adding even numbers doesn't affect parity). Wait, the sum of numbers in a row is K, which is even. Therefore, the sum mod 2 is 0. The sum mod 2 is equal to the number of odd entries mod 2, because each even number contributes 0 mod 2, and each odd number contributes 1. So the number of odd entries in each row must be even. Similarly, each column must also have an even number of odd entries.\n\nTherefore, in the entire grid, the total number of odd entries must be even, s