\]\n\nNow, the RHS becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 1}{7} \\times a^3 \\times \\left[ (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta) + \\sin^2 \\theta (\\cos \\theta + 1) + \\cos^2 \\theta (1 + \\sin \\theta) \\right]\n\\]\n\nSo the inequality simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\sin^3 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta \\geq \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 1}{7} \\left[ (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta) + \\sin^2 \\theta (\\cos \\theta + 1) + \\cos^2 \\theta (1 + \\sin \\theta) \\right]\n\\]\n\nSince \\( a^3 \\) is positive, we can divide both sides by \\( a^3 \\), which cancels out, leaving an inequality in terms of \\( \\theta \\).\n\nSo now, the problem reduces to proving this trigonometric inequality. Let me compute the expressions inside the brackets and then see if I can simplify further.\n\nFirst, let's compute the bracketed term on the RHS:\n\nLet me denote:\n\n\\( S = (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta) + \\sin^2 \\theta (\\cos \\theta + 1) + \\cos^2 \\theta (1 + \\sin \\theta) \\)\n\nExpand the terms:\n\nFirst term: \\( \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( \\sin^2 \\theta \\cos \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta \\)\n\nThird term: \\( \\cos^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta \\sin \\theta \\)\n\nSo adding all together:\n\n\\( \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta \\cos \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta \\sin \\theta \\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n- The \\( \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1 \\)\n- The \\( \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta \\) remains\n- The \\( \\sin^2 \\theta \\cos \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta \\sin \\theta = \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta) \\)\n\nTherefore, S becomes:\n\n\\( 1 + \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta) \\)\n\nHmm, that's a bit better. Let me write that:\n\n\\( S = 1 + \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta) \\)\n\nNow, the LHS is:\n\n\\( 1 + \\sin^3 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta \\)\n\nRecall that \\( \\sin^3 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta = (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta)(1 - \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta) \\), using the identity \\( x^3 + y^3 = (x + y)(x^2 - xy + y^2) \\).\n\nSo, substituting that into LHS:\n\n\\( 1 + (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta)(1 - \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta) \\)\n\nTherefore, the inequality becomes:\n\n\\[\n1 + (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta)(1 - \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta) \\geq \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 1}{7} \\left[ 1 + \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta) \\right]\n\\]\n\nLet me denote \\( t = \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta \\). Then, since \\( \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta = \\frac{t^2 - 1}{2} \\), because:\n\n\\( (\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta)^2 = \\sin^2 \\theta + 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1 + 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\), so \\( t^2 = 1 + 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\implies \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta = \\frac{t^2 - 1}{2} \\).\n\nTherefore, replacing \\( \\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta \\) with \\( t \\), and \\( \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\) with \\( \\frac{t^2 - 1}{2} \\), the inequality can be rewritten in terms of \\( t \\).\n\nFirst, let's express the LHS:\n\n\\( 1 + t \\left(1 - \\frac{t^2 - 1}{2}\\right) \\)\n\nSimplify the term inside the parentheses:\n\n\\( 1 - \\frac{t^2 - 1}{2} = \\frac{2 - t^2 + 1}{2} = \\frac{3 - t^2}{2} \\)\n\nSo LHS becomes:\n\n\\( 1 + t \\times \\frac{3 - t^2}{2} = 1 + \\frac{3t - t^3}{2} \\)\n\nSimilarly, the RHS expression inside the brackets is:\n\n\\( 1 + t + \\frac{t^2 - 1}{2} \\times t \\)\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: 1 + t\n\nSecond term: \\( \\frac{t^2 - 1}{2} \\times t = \\frac{t^3 - t}{2} \\)\n\nTherefore, the entire RHS expression inside the brackets is:\n\n\\( 1 + t + \\frac{t^3 - t}{2} = 1 + t + \\frac{t^3}{2} - \\frac{t}{2} = 1 + \\frac{t}{2} + \\frac{t^3}{2} \\)\n\nTherefore, the RHS of the inequality is:\n\n\\( \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 1}{7} \\times \\left(1 + \\frac{t}{2} + \\frac{t^3}{2}\\right) \\)\n\