\\sqrt{x} \\)), the only solution is:\n   \\[\n   y = 2\\sqrt{x}\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe side of the triangle \\( y \\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\sqrt{x}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's denote the projections of points $C$ and $B$ onto the line $AM$ as $C_1$ and $B_1$, respectively. Then, we get the following diagram:\n\n2. Based on the properties of triangles and projections, we have:\n    \\[\n    4\\left(S_{AMB} + S_{AMC}\\right) = 2 \\cdot AM \\cdot \\left(BB_1 + CC_1\\right)\n    \\]\n    Given that $BB_1 + CC_1 \\leq BC$, we can write:\n    \\[\n    4\\left(S_{AMB} + S_{AMC}\\right) \\leq 2 \\cdot AM \\cdot BC\n    \\]\n3. Using the property of the areas and the fact that the sum of the areas is less than or equal to the right-hand side, we have:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} ( \\sqrt{3} \\cdot AM \\cdot BC ) \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} (3AM^2 + BC^2)\n    \\]\n4. Similarly, using the same logic for other sides of the triangle:\n    \\[\n    4\\left(S_{BMC} + S_{BMA}\\right) \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} (3BM^2 + AC^2)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    4\\left(S_{CMA} + S_{CMB}\\right) \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} (3CM^2 + AB^2)\n    \\]\n5. Combining these results, we get:\n    \\[\n    8 \\sqrt{3} S_{ABC} \\leq AB^2 + AC^2 + BC^2 + 3 ( AM^2 + BM^2 + CM^2 )\n    \\]\n6. To verify that equality holds only in the case of an equilateral triangle, we need to prove that all angles are $60^\\circ$. Consider the angle $\\beta$ in the triangle:\n    \\[\n    AC = BM \\sqrt{3} \\Longleftrightarrow b = \\sqrt{3} x\n    \\]\n    From the triangle $AON$, we have:\n    \\[\n    \\tan \\beta = \\frac{AN}{ON} = \\frac{b/2}{x/2} = \\sqrt{3} \\Longleftrightarrow \\beta = 60^\\circ \n    \\]\n    Consequently, all angles of the triangle $ABC$ are $60^\\circ$, confirming that it is equilateral.\n\n7. Further, it is given that:\n    \\[\n    8 \\sqrt{3} S_{ABC} \\geq 24 x \\geq AB^2 + AC^2 + BC^2 + 3(AM^2 + BM^2 + CM^2)\n    \\]\n    Hence, the triangle $ABC$ is equilateral. 