1C_1} = \\frac{a}{t} \\cdot \\frac{d}{t} \\cdot \\frac{c}{t} \\cdot V = \\frac{adc}{t^3}V.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Comparing Volumes:**\n   We know the total volume of the smaller pyramids formed should equate to the entire pyramid's partial volumes:\n   \\[\n   V_{PA_1B_1C_1} + V_{PA_1D_1C_1} = \\frac{abc}{t^3}V + \\frac{adc}{t^3}V = \\frac{V}{t^3} (abc + adc).\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly:\n   \\[\n   V_{PA_1B_1D_1} + V_{PB_1C_1D_1} = \\frac{abd}{t^3}V + \\frac{bcd}{t^3}V = \\frac{V}{t^3} (abd + bcd).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Equality of Volumes:**\n   By comparing the two equations obtained from the volume sums:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{V}{t^3} (abc + adc) = \\frac{V}{t^3} (abd + bcd).\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplification:**\n   Since \\(V\\) and \\(t^3\\) are non-zero, we can cancel them out:\n   \\[\n   abc + adc = abd + bcd.\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing both sides by \\(abcd\\) to simplify:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{bcd} + \\frac{1}{a} = \\frac{1}{acd} + \\frac{1}{b}.\n   \\]\n\n   Further simplifying \\(bcd\\) and \\(acd\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{d} \\left( \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{c} \\right) = \\frac{1}{a} \\left( \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d} \\right).\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion:**\n   This establishes that:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{d} = \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c}.\n   \\]\n\nThus we have shown that:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{c} = \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{d}}.\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nIs it possible to arrange the numbers \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, 10 \\) in a sequence in such a way that each number, starting from the second one, differs from the previous one by an integer percentage?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if I can arrange the numbers 1 through 10 in a sequence where each number after the first differs from the previous one by an integer percentage. Let me try to understand the problem first.\n\nHmm, integer percentage. That means the difference between consecutive numb